        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 716,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 798,
            "height": 716,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "sig~ brings a control value into the audio graph, gliding smoothly to  each new value.\n\nIt is the entry point for effort data and for anything else that arrives as  messages - a slider, a 60 Hz sensor stream, a computed number. Patched  straight into a gain or a pitch, a stream like that steps, and you hear each  step as a zipper or a click. sig~ turns it into a continuous signal: each  new value is approached smoothly rather than jumped to.\n\nGLIDE:\n'glide' is in seconds: how long it takes to cover about two thirds of the  way to a new value. It closes the rest of the gap ever more slowly, so it is  effectively there after about three glides. The default, 0.02, is just long  enough to stop a zipper; longer glides become audible portamento, as in the  demo. 0 jumps straight to each value.\n\nSCALE AND OFFSET:\nThe value is multiplied by 'scale' and has 'offset' added before it glides,  so a 0 to 1 control can become, say, a -1 to 1 swing without another node.\n\nSYNTAX:\nsig~ <value>\n\nEXAMPLE:\nsig~ 0.5\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nvalue:\nThe control value. Send it numbers, or drag it. The argument sets the  starting value, which the output glides up to from 0 as the patch starts.\n\nglide:\nSeconds to cover about two thirds of a change. Default 0.02.\n\nscale / offset:\nThe output is value times scale, plus offset. Defaults 1 and 0.\n\nenable:\nOff fades out over a few milliseconds, then stops computing.\n\nOUTPUTS: \n\nsignal:\nThe smoothed signal.\n\nRELATED:\nsnapshot~ goes the other way: a signal's value back out as a number. ramp~ moves to each value in a straight line over a set time.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 790,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 700,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "sig~ brings a control value into the audio graph, gliding smoothly to  each new value.\n\nIt is the entry point for effort data and for anything else that arrives as  messages - a slider, a 60 Hz sensor stream, a computed number. Patched  straight into a gain or a pitch, a stream like that steps, and you hear each  step as a zipper or a click. sig~ turns it into a continuous signal: each  new value is approached smoothly rather than jumped to.\n\nGLIDE:\n'glide' is in seconds: how long it takes to cover about two thirds of the  way to a new value. It closes the rest of the gap ever more slowly, so it is  effectively there after about three glides. The default, 0.02, is just long  enough to stop a zipper; longer glides become audible portamento, as in the  demo. 0 jumps straight to each value.\n\nSCALE AND OFFSET:\nThe value is multiplied by 'scale' and has 'offset' added before it glides,  so a 0 to 1 control can become, say, a -1 to 1 swing without another node.\n\nSYNTAX:\nsig~ <value>\n\nEXAMPLE:\nsig~ 0.5\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nvalue:\nThe control value. Send it numbers, or drag it. The argument sets the  starting value, which the output glides up to from 0 as the patch starts.\n\nglide:\nSeconds to cover about two thirds of a change. Default 0.02.\n\nscale / offset:\nThe output is value times scale, plus offset. Defaults 1 and 0.\n\nenable:\nOff fades out over a few milliseconds, then stops computing.\n\nOUTPUTS: \n\nsignal:\nThe smoothed signal.\n\nRELATED:\nsnapshot~ goes the other way: a signal's value back out as a number. ramp~ moves to each value in a straight line over a set time."
        },
